A DBMS manages stored data according to its data model and interfaces.
SQL is used to define, retrieve, insert, update, and delete relational data.
SQL accepted by one database system is not guaranteed to work identically on another.
Schema describes structure.
A primary key identifies a row according to the table definition.
A key containing multiple columns is commonly called a composite key.
Key selection should follow data identity requirements.
Referential integrity prevents references from violating defined relationships.
Constraints place important invariants near the data.
NULL represents the absence of a value according to SQL semantics.
NULL is not the same as zero.
NULL is not the same as an empty string.
A comparison such as value = NULL does not normally test for NULL.
A SELECT statement retrieves data.
A WHERE clause filters rows according to its predicate.
A SELECT result has no guaranteed row order merely because rows previously appeared in a particular order.
