LANGUAGE: English (American)
BOUNDARY: Similar words are not automatically interchangeable; interpret meaning in context.
ENTRY 001: who — asks for a person. Example: Who called?
ENTRY 004: where — asks for a location. Example: Where is the file?
ENTRY 007: which — asks for a selection. Example: Which module failed?
ENTRY 015: describe — state characteristics. Example: Describe the module.
ENTRY 018: identify — establish what something is. Example: Identify the source.
ENTRY 020: review — examine for assessment. Example: Review the report.
ENTRY 030: search — look systematically. Example: Search the Corpus.
ENTRY 031: retrieve — obtain stored information. Example: Retrieve the record.
ENTRY 037: compile — translate source into executable form. Example: Compile the program.
ENTRY 040: stop — end operation. Example: Stop the service.
ENTRY 052: ingest — process information into a system. Example: Ingest the lesson.
ENTRY 053: learn — acquire knowledge or skill. Example: Learn the vocabulary.
ENTRY 055: forget — cease retaining information. Example: Forget the temporary example.
ENTRY 057: reply — respond to a message. Example: Reply briefly.
ENTRY 060: notify — send a notice. Example: Notify the operator.
ENTRY 067: choose — select among options. Example: Choose one result.
ENTRY 072: assume — accept without proof for a stated purpose. Example: Do not assume success.
ENTRY 077: intend — have a purpose in mind. Example: What did the operator intend?
ENTRY 087: observe — notice or record. Example: Observe the output.
ENTRY 094: and — joins items or conditions. Example: Read and verify.
ENTRY 097: because — introduces a reason. Example: It failed because the file was missing.
ENTRY 103: until — marks an ending condition. Example: Wait until complete.
ENTRY 104: while — indicates simultaneous action or contrast. Example: Read while monitoring.
ENTRY 116: below — lower than. Example: The value is below the limit.
ENTRY 131: than — introduces comparison. Example: This is faster than that.
ENTRY 137: none — means not one. Example: None passed.
ENTRY 142: only — restricts scope. Example: Only the operator may approve.
ENTRY 144: every — includes each member. Example: Every test passed.
ENTRY 146: all — includes the whole group. Example: All modules loaded.
ENTRY 148: many — indicates a large number. Example: Many records matched.
ENTRY 153: less — indicates smaller amount. Example: Less time was used.
ENTRY 155: enough — indicates sufficiency. Example: There is enough evidence.
ENTRY 159: now — at the present time. Example: Check now.
ENTRY 182: inactive — not currently active. Example: The module is inactive.
ENTRY 185: pending — awaiting completion or decision. Example: Approval is pending.
ENTRY 195: correct — free of the relevant error. Example: The checksum is correct.
ENTRY 199: exact — precisely matching. Example: Use an exact match.
ENTRY 201: certain — supported without relevant doubt. Example: The result is certain.
ENTRY 209: evidence — information supporting a claim. Example: Show the evidence.
ENTRY 210: proof — evidence establishing a conclusion under a standard. Example: Provide proof of integrity.
ENTRY 213: opinion — a judgment or preference. Example: That is an opinion.
ENTRY 217: inference — a conclusion drawn from evidence. Example: Label the inference.
ENTRY 220: reference — information used for consultation. Example: Cite the reference.
ENTRY 221: context — surrounding information affecting meaning. Example: Read the context.
ENTRY 223: irrelevant — not connected to the question. Example: Exclude irrelevant records.
ENTRY 228: consistent — not contradictory. Example: The results are consistent.
ENTRY 238: response — a reply to a request. Example: The response was returned.
ENTRY 248: lesson — structured information for ingestion. Example: Ingest the lesson.
ENTRY 250: directory — a container for files. Example: Check the directory.
ENTRY 253: authority — legitimate scope for decisions or actions. Example: Remain within authority.
ENTRY 256: boundary — a limit of allowed behavior. Example: Respect the boundary.
ENTRY 258: failure — an unsuccessful outcome. Example: Investigate the failure.
ENTRY 262: event — an occurrence of interest. Example: Log the event.
ENTRY 263: trace — a record of processing steps. Example: Inspect the trace.
ENTRY 268: commit — a recorded change in version control. Example: Inspect the commit.
ENTRY 269: repository — a managed collection of source files. Example: Open the repository.
ENTRY 271: binary — machine-readable executable or data. Example: Load the binary.
ENTRY 272: compiler — a program that translates source code. Example: Run the compiler.
ENTRY 273: runtime — the environment or period of execution. Example: Inspect runtime behavior.
ENTRY 275: protocol — rules governing communication. Example: Follow the protocol.
ENTRY 279: timeout — a limit on waiting duration. Example: The request timed out.
ENTRY 287: qualification — evidence-based confirmation of readiness. Example: Qualification passed.
SECTION: CONVERSATION AND SOCIAL
ENTRY 288: hello — a greeting. Example: Hello, Digit.
ENTRY 289: hi — an informal greeting. Example: Hi there.
ENTRY 300: maybe — expression of uncertainty. Example: Maybe the file is missing.
ENTRY 301: perhaps — expression of possibility. Example: Perhaps the service stopped.
ENTRY 302: probably — expression of likelihood. Example: It probably timed out.
ENTRY 305: exactly — agreement with precision. Example: Exactly, that is the result.
ENTRY 308: hold on — request to wait. Example: Hold on before restarting.
ENTRY 310: no worries — informal reassurance. Example: No worries, try again.
ENTRY 319: could you — often introduces a polite request. Example: Could you explain this?
SECTION: POLYSEMY AND CONTEXT
ENTRY 324: right — correct; direction; entitlement. Example: Right answer; turn right; access rights.
ENTRY 328: file — stored document; submit formally; tool for smoothing. Example: Open a file; file a report.
ENTRY 335: table — structured data; furniture. Example: Database table; dining table.
ENTRY 344: load — bring into memory; weight or burden. Example: Load the module; heavy load.
ENTRY 351: up — operational; higher position. Example: Server is up; look up.
SECTION: IDIOMS AND INFORMAL ENGLISH
ENTRY 358: hit the nail on the head — describe something exactly correctly. Example: You hit the nail on the head.
ENTRY 363: ballpark figure — rough estimate. Example: Give me a ballpark figure.
ENTRY 365: green light — permission or approval in context. Example: The operator gave the green light.
ENTRY 366: hold your horses — wait or slow down. Example: Hold your horses; check first.
ENTRY 368: give me a hand — help me. Example: Give me a hand with this.
ENTRY 371: out of the blue — unexpectedly. Example: The failure came out of the blue.
ENTRY 374: drop the ball — fail to perform a responsibility. Example: We dropped the ball on testing.
SECTION: APPLICATION PRINCIPLES
Negation changes meaning and must be preserved exactly.
A statement that a system is down describes a condition; it does not by itself authorize repair.
Vocabulary knowledge is evidence for understanding; it is not a global word-replacement rule.
