Memory management is the control of memory used by a program.
A program should not assume unlimited memory.
Memory allocation can fail.
Memory exhaustion can affect other processes.
A memory leak occurs when memory remains allocated after the program no longer needs it and the program has lost or intentionally retained the ability to release it.
A memory leak does not necessarily cause immediate failure.
Freeing referenced memory can invalidate every pointer that depends on that memory.
A null pointer should not be dereferenced.
Pointer validity depends on provenance, lifetime, bounds, alignment, and language rules.
