SQL is not a general-purpose programming language in the same sense as C, C++, Go, or JavaScript.
PostgreSQL uses SQL.
SQLite uses SQL.
MySQL is a relational database management system that implements an SQL dialect.
A database schema describes database structures.
Database identifier rules vary between SQL implementations.
SELECT retrieves data.
INSERT adds rows.
UPDATE modifies existing rows.
DELETE removes rows.
CREATE defines database objects.
SELECT * requests all columns from the selected source.
NULL is not equal to itself using ordinary equality comparison.
Comparisons involving NULL commonly produce UNKNOWN.
Deterministic result ordering requires an ORDER BY expression sufficient to distinguish ordering as required.
Keyset pagination can provide an alternative for appropriate ordered datasets.
Aggregate functions compute values from sets of rows.
MAX returns a maximum value.
A GROUP BY query produces results according to grouping expressions.
HAVING filters groups after grouping.
Tables can be joined even without a declared foreign key.
A primary key uniquely identifies rows according to its constraint.
A primary key can consist of one column.
A primary key can consist of multiple columns.
A multi-column primary key is a composite primary key.
Primary keys and UNIQUE constraints are related but not identical concepts.
Foreign keys enforce referential integrity.
NO ACTION has semantics defined by the database and constraint timing.
Constraints enforce data rules within the database.
NOT NULL prevents NULL values in a column.
DEFAULT supplies a default value when an insert does not provide one according to statement and database semantics.
BIGINT represents a larger-range integer type.
FLOAT and REAL commonly represent approximate floating-point values.
CHAR represents fixed-length character data according to implementation semantics.
DROP TABLE removes a table definition and its data according to database semantics.
Schema-changing operations should be controlled.
A migration represents an intentional change from one database schema state to another.
Schema changes can lock tables or consume substantial resources depending on the database and operation.
INSERT adds rows to a table.
INSERT behavior for generated columns and defaults depends on the schema and database.
The SET clause specifies new column values.
The WHERE clause restricts rows removed by DELETE.
DELETE and DROP are different operations.
READ UNCOMMITTED permits weak isolation in systems supporting it.
READ COMMITTED prevents certain dirty reads according to database semantics.
A non-repeatable read occurs when repeated reads can observe a changed committed value.
Serialization anomalies involve concurrent executions producing outcomes inconsistent with required serializable behavior.
A database can use predicate, range, metadata, or other lock forms depending on implementation.
Database systems can detect and resolve deadlocks by aborting a transaction.
Retrying a failed transaction can be appropriate when the operation is safe to retry.
Sending an email and committing a SQL transaction are not one atomic operation merely because application code performs them together.
Distributed transaction requirements need explicit architecture.
More indexes are not automatically better.
An index on columns A and B is not universally equivalent to separate indexes on A and B.
Execution plans should be examined when query performance matters.
Stale or inaccurate statistics can influence plan quality.
Query performance is affected by schema, indexes, data distribution, query form, concurrency, memory, storage, and database implementation.
Normalization organizes relational data to reduce undesirable redundancy and dependency problems.
First Normal Form is commonly abbreviated 1NF.
Denormalization can improve some read patterns.
A denormalized design can still be correct if redundancy is deliberately maintained.
A one-to-one relationship associates one row with at most one corresponding row according to defined constraints.
A view is a stored query definition presented as a table-like object.
A materialized view stores query results in systems supporting materialized views.
Views can simplify repeated query logic.
Views can provide an abstraction over underlying schema.
Database-side logic should be deliberate and documented.
Parameterized queries separate SQL structure from supplied data values according to the database API.
A parameter does not ordinarily represent an arbitrary column name.
