A valid query can still expose data the user is not authorized to access.
Least privilege limits the damage possible through compromised credentials or application defects.
A web application generally should not connect as a database administrative superuser for ordinary requests.
Application authorization controls what application actors can do.
Encryption at rest does not prevent an authorized running application from accessing data it is permitted to decrypt or read.
An accidental DELETE can replicate to replicas.
Applications should handle constraint violations.
Applications should handle transaction failures.
Operational logs can preserve diagnostic information according to security policy.
A database connection is a finite resource.
A pool has finite capacity.
A leaked connection can exhaust a connection pool.
Connection timeout and query timeout are different concepts.
A query or command timeout limits execution waiting according to the client and database.
Reading data is generally safer to retry than performing an unprotected state-changing operation.
